## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a compact ranking structure with duplicates and a fixed value range, using O(log U) updates and O(log U) kth-order queries.

## Key Concepts

- Fenwick tree for prefix sums
- Binary lifting to find k-th order statistic (standard BIT trick: find largest power of 2 <= n, then iterate downward checking cumulative tree values)
- Handling duplicates via per-value counts

## Code Example

```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n) { 
    this.n = n; 
    this.tree = new Array(n + 1).fill(0); 
  }

  add(i, delta) {
    for (; i <= this.n; i += i & -i) this.tree[i] += delta;
  }

  sum(i) {
    let s = 0;
    for (; i > 0; i -= i & -i) s += this.tree[i];
    return s;
  }

  kth(k) {
    if (k < 1) return -1;
    const total = this.sum(this.n);
    if (k > total) return -1;

    let idx = 0;
    let bit = 1;
    while (bit << 1 <= this.n) bit <<= 1;

    let sum = 0;
    for (; bit > 0; bit >>= 1) {
      const next = idx + bit;
      if (next <= this.n && sum + this.tree[next] < k) {
        sum += this.tree[next];
        idx = next;
      }
    }
    return idx; // 0-indexed
  }
}

class Leaderboard {
  constructor(maxScore = 1000000) {
    this.bit = new Fenwick(maxScore + 1);
  }

  insertScore(s) { this.bit.add(s + 1, 1); }

  eraseScore(s) { this.bit.add(s + 1, -1); }

  getKthScore(k) { return this.bit.kth(k) - 1; }

  getRank(s) { return this.bit.sum(s + 1); }
}
```

## Handling Ties

Since each score has its own count, duplicates naturally work:
- insertScore(50) adds count at index 50
- insertScore(50) again increments same index
- eraseScore(50) decrements it
- getRank(50) returns all scores <= 50 including duplicates
- getKthScore(k) skips to the k-th element accounting for counts

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to combine a circular buffer with a monotonic deque to support rolling-window stats efficiently; checks edge-cases with duplicates and partial windows.

## Key Concepts

- Circular buffers
- Monotonic deque for max
- Running sum
- Amortized analysis

## Code Example

```javascript
class RollingWindow {
  constructor(n) {
    this.n = n;
    this.buf = new Array(n);
    this.head = 0; // points to oldest element
    this.size = 0;
    this.sum = 0;
    this.maxDQ = []; // decreasing values
  }
  push(v) {
    // evict oldest if full
    if (this.size === this.n) {
      const old = this.buf[this.head];
      this.sum -= old;
      if (this.maxDQ[0] === old) this.maxDQ.shift();
      this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.n;
      this.size--;
    }
    const idx = (this.head + this.size) % this.n;
    this.buf[idx] = v;
    this.size++;
    this.sum += v;
    while (this.maxDQ.length && this.maxDQ[this.maxDQ.length - 1] < v) this.maxDQ.pop();
    this.maxDQ.push(v);
  }
  sumWindow() {
    return this.sum;
  }
  maxWindow() {
    return this.maxDQ.length ? this.maxDQ[0] : undefined;
  }
}
```

